# KaminIDE patches — taffy 0.14.0 (DioxusLabs/taffy 2d936b7701f64b0fe95e50f1bfdcfc65a9f88f6f, cargo package)
#
# 1. Deep layout: block, flex and grid descendant queries use a shared heap work stack through borrowed leaf measurements; public synchronous entry points drive the same algorithms.
# 2. Tree traversal: round final geometry with explicit child cursors, visiting in-flow then hoisted children relative to their containing block; invalidate ancestors iteratively up to the first empty cache; clear hidden owned subtrees with remaining-sibling iterators without changing the partial-tree API; scan forwarded percentage-height dependencies with explicit sibling cursors and unchanged pruning. These passes consume bounded native stack regardless of tree depth.
# 3. Intrinsic queries: expose the existing cached ComputeSize traversal for a requested axis without performing final root layout.
# 4. Upstream #1353: retain the used physical relative-position vector separately from Layout.location in block, flex, grid and lanes, including vertical block-flow conversion.
# 5. Native stack depth: construct already-boxed per-container flex constants in a separate non-inlined preparation frame before descending into children, and keep the non-inlined logical-axis adapter out of the identity fast path when parent and children already use the physical horizontal LTR coordinate system. Complete output adaptation, out-of-flow placement and cache cloning after recursive container layout returns, in separate native frames. Intrinsic measurements retain margin-collapse metadata so empty deep blocks remain cacheable.
# 6. Export final flex lines and item geometry; preserve flex sizing while applying fragmentation consumed space on either physical block axis and in either direction, retaining used automatic minima for scroll/monolithic items (CSS Flexbox 1 section 10; Writing Modes 3 logical-to-physical mappings). Line membership fixed before fragmentation is addressed by the exported line indices in both wrap directions. Negative row line growth suppresses the gap at a break after alignment: later lines move towards block-start and only a content-sized container shrinks (css-gaps-1 section 2).
# 7. Grid fragmentation: block space inserted after the tracks of one physical axis keeps unfragmented track sizing and content alignment, moves following tracks, grows spanning areas and a content-sized container, and reaches subgrids inside their span (CSS Grid 2 sections 9 and 12).
# 8. Leaf measurement returns baselines so flex/grid baseline alignment uses text metrics.
# 9. CSS sizing: intrinsic constraints, aspect ratios, box sizing and percentage resolution.
# 10. Flex/grid/block layout: carry CSS margins, baselines and track sizing through layout.
# 11. Grid/subgrid/lanes: retain local track sizing, masonry and physical baseline algorithms with their regression fixtures.
# 12. Intrinsic limits and out-of-flow layout: preserve containing sizes and CSS min/max constraints across measurement passes.
# 13. Upstream #1293: borrow OOF candidate slices while retaining lazy ownership and hoisted-child lifecycle.
# 14. Upstream #1322/#1323: export final border-box baselines independently of grouping margins and relative paint offsets; preserve first/last physical X/Y channels and scroll clamping.
# 15. Upstream #1242/#1303/#1305/#1304: stretch edge-adjoining margins, float identity/readback, relative painting and complete exclusion bands.
# 16. Upstream #1320/#1335: accept length-percentage calc in auto-repeat tracks and count outer flex margins once; retain compressible controls per CSS Sizing 3 instead of #1351.
# 17. Upstream #1327/#1328/#1333/#1337/#1336/#1332: avoid temporary allocation and unused stretch probes while preserving final metadata, baseline groups and percentage bases.
# 18. Packaging: retain the selected source/examples; omit the upstream generated test module.
# 19. calc-size: resolve retained keyword bases separately for intrinsic contributions and used sizes; preserve CSS automatic minima.
# 20. Node storage: node_storage_full and reserve_nodes let a host grow the node, children and parent storage once to an expected node count.
# 21. Shared node styles: a node holds its Style behind SharedStyle (Rc, as Style is not Send) so equal styles of a tree may share one allocation (new_leaf/new_with_children/set_style accept Style or SharedStyle, shared_style hands it out); subgrid tracks are boxed.
# 22. Retained trees: retain_nodes drops unkept nodes in one sweep, reset_node reuses a node's storage for a fresh node, children_slice/has_node_context read without allocating, and style_equals/adopt_equal_style compare and swap in an equal shared style without clearing the node's cache.
# 23. Lazy node storage: the size-only measure entries of a node's cache are allocated by its first measurement and kept by clear; the final (rounded) layout is allocated only by rounding, a tree without rounding reads the unrounded layout.
